# The headline is the dependent this time, so the runner announces jq
# first and jq's install pulls its sibling out of the staged overlay
# and builds it as an ordinary dependency. oniguruma breaks there,
# under jq's marker, having never been announced as a subject at all.
#
# This is the case that decides whether the blame reader is looking at
# the change's roster or only at the port whose section it is reading.
# A reader asking "is the port named here the one this section belongs
# to" gets no for jq, calls oniguruma a dependency, and blocks jq on a
# stranger — handing back the environment the failure was supposed to
# keep, and recording nothing at all against the member that actually
# broke. The roster answers instead: oniguruma is one of us, so this is
# our failure, and the guest stays.
#
# It is the marked twin of TestABlockedSubjectBlamesItsSibling, which
# drives the same shape through a log with no markers in it.
# SYNTHESIZED from the cohort runner in internal/verify/tart/tart.go,
# not captured: see README.md.
members: jq oniguruma
outcome: failed
jq.state: blocked
jq.blamed: oniguruma
jq.detail: oniguruma fails to build; this member is untested
oniguruma.state: failed
oniguruma.detail: Failed to build oniguruma: command execution failed
